## 1. The high-t use of `q` has the correct direction

The post-exclusion survivor statement is

\[
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge1.844.
\]

Canonical `k=d_H+s_Z>=0`, so pointwise

\[
q\ge q-k.
\]

Therefore

\[
\liminf q/D\ge1.844.
\]

No upper/lower liminf reversal occurs.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 2. `J_D=2T-P_F` is exact

With

\[
J_D=(b-P_F)+(Q+4f),
\]

canonical outer/train bookkeeping gives exactly

\[
J_D=2T-P_F.
\]

Also

\[
2T=q+2(T-q/2).
\]

Hence

\[
\liminf {J_D\over D}
\ge1.844+2(2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.007707)-\pi.
\]

The signs are all favorable in the asserted direction: `q` and `T-q/2` are lower
bounded, while `P_F/D->pi` is an actual limit.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 3. The `H_D>=3J_D/5` conversion is canonical and correctly oriented

Put

\[
d=b-P_F\ge0,
\qquad
J_D=d+Q+4f,
\qquad
H_D=d+Q+2f.
\]

Every bounded nonquadrilateral face has degree at least five, so `Q>=f`. Therefore

\[
5H_D-3J_D=2(d+Q-f)\ge0.
\]

Thus

\[
H_D\ge3J_D/5.
\]

This is the same exact Section-85 inequality already used and audited in the older
correlated-interface stack. The candidate does not identify global `Q+4f` with any
local induced-cell perimeter.

**Verdict:** PASS.

